# Sound: the test machines have no sound card, so the commands say so and
# check what they are given. (The HD Audio driver was tried by hand on
# QEMU's intel-hda with a WAV recording of its output, and on the HP.)
$ sound
sound: no sound card (HD Audio) found
$ beep
beep: no sound card (HD Audio) found
$ beep a4 200
beep: no sound card (HD Audio) found
$ beep h4
usage: beep [HZ|NOTE [MS]]   (e.g. beep 440 500, beep c5; 20-20000 Hz, at most 10000 ms)
$ beep 440 0
usage: beep [HZ|NOTE [MS]]   (e.g. beep 440 500, beep c5; 20-20000 Hz, at most 10000 ms)
$ sound volume 101
usage: sound volume 0-100
$ sound volume 50
sound: no sound card (HD Audio) found
$ play
usage: play FILE.wav
$ play /missing.wav
play /missing.wav: no such file or directory
$ echo hello > /note.txt
$ play /note.txt
play /note.txt: not a WAV file
# A tune saved as a WAV file and read back (playing needs a card).
$ tune save /tune.wav c5 e5 g5 c6:500 r:100
saved 5 notes to /tune.wav (254 KiB)
$ play /tune.wav
/tune.wav: 48000 Hz, 16 bits, stereo, 1.4 s (Ctrl+C stops)
play: no sound card (HD Audio) found
$ tune c5 e5
tune: no sound card (HD Audio) found
$ tune save /tune.txt c5
usage: tune save FILE.wav NOTE[:MS]...
$ tune c5 x5
tune: not a note (like c5, a#4, 440, or r for a rest)
$ tune
usage: tune NOTE[:MS]...  or  tune save FILE.wav NOTE[:MS]...   (e.g. tune c5 e5 g5 c6:500; r is a rest)
